// @check
// @error Codegen error
// FALSE-REJECT xfail (ROADMAP A5 / A6). This program is SOUND and SHOULD compile
// and run; it is rejected. The `xfail-false-reject-` prefix is the corpus's
// rejects-too-much kind — see README.vl. NOT a soundness hole: nothing unsound is
// accepted, a valid program is refused.
//
// THE GAP: a *sequence* of two divergent guard-clauses over a 3-way value union
// narrows the fall-through to the last remaining variant — here `x` is `boolean`
// after subtracting `i32` then `string`. Neither backend can materialize the
// twice-subtracted residual of a tagged value union, so `postGuardNarrowings`
// applies only ONE union subtraction per place per block and the front end
// rejects the second (`typecheck.vl`, the GUARD CLAUSE comment). (The
// single-subtraction case — one guard over a 2-way union — works: see
// is-value-kind-union-sound.vl, narrowing-null-guard.vl.)
//
// EXPECTED: compiles + runs, `f(true)` returns `true`.
// ACTUAL: the native checker rejects with `return type mismatch: expected
// boolean, got string | boolean`. The `@error Codegen error` text below is the
// retired TS host's wording, which is why this case sits in
// `EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES` in tests/cases_wasm_test.ts rather than being
// adjudicated by the wasm oracle.
//
// TODO(completeness): handle the multi-step union residual in the narrowed-type
// unbox path, then let `postGuardNarrowings` subtract more than once. When fixed,
// flip to `// @run` + `// @log true`, drop @error, drop the divergence entry, and
// drop the `xfail-false-reject-` prefix.
function f(x: string | i32 | boolean): boolean {
  if x is i32 { return false }
  if x is string { return false }
  return x
}
print(f(true))
